e  individuals who want to cut back if  they're drinking a lot of sugar  sweetened beverages this study did look  at artificially sweetened beverages and  found that there was no real increase in  mortality unless you drank more than  four of these drinks a day and you were  a woman and so they need more research  to look into that female effect it may  have been that women who were overweight  or obese were trying to switch to  artificial sweetened beverages and they  were already at high risk so why was it  higher in women do you think it's  because of weight is that what you're  saying so this research in general in  terms of the sugar sweetened beverages  did show an effect that was higher in  women and the researchers were quick to  point out that we don't  know why it could be that the biology of  women is different how we metabolize  these drinks are different our  physiology or it could be the  methodology of the study that women  tended to report their drinking use  differently from men the American  Beverage Association did not respond to  our request for comment but their  websites is that soft drinks are safe as  part of a balanced diet do you agree  with you know the lead researcher even  of this study said look if you're  drinking one or two soft drinks a week  probably the incremental increase in  risk is very small but what we know is  that sugary sweetened beverages are the  leading cause of added sugar in the  American diet it says even cocktails at  night right that have sugary these  sports and energy drinks the fact of the  matter is Americans are should be  consuming for men about nine teaspoons  of added sugar a day women six teaspoons  on average we consume 20 teaspoons of  sugar a day when you look at a 12 ounce  soda for example that has about 10  teaspoons so with one soda  you've already over gone over what you  should have you know consumed for that  day what are we left with boring water  pouring water boring okay healthy water  fresh w